.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KILL 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm kill
.Nd send a signal to processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s Ar signal_name
.Ar pid ...
.Nm
.Fl l
.Op Ar exit_status
.Nm
.Fl Ar signal_name
.Ar pid ...
.Nm
.Fl Ar signal_number
.Ar pid ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends a signal, by default
.Dv SIGTERM ,
to each
.Ar pid .
A negative
.Ar pid
names a process group; write it after
.Fl -
or after the signal, so that it isn't read as an option.
Signals are named with or without
.Dq SIG ,
in any case, or given by number; 0 sends nothing, but checks that the process
exists and may be signalled.
.Pp
.Fl l
writes the names of the signals; with an
.Ar exit_status ,
the name of the signal that ended a process with that status
.Pq for example 143, Dv SIGTERM .
.Pp
Shells have a
.Ic kill
built-in that also takes job numbers
.Pq Li %1 ;
this is the program, for use where a shell isn't.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 if every signal was sent, 1 if any wasn't, 2 on a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask process 123 to terminate, then make sure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kill 123
kill -KILL 123
.Ed
.Pp
Hang up every process in group 42:
.Dl kill -s HUP -- -42
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr signal 3
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v3 .
